Let $X$ be an open subset of $\mathbb R^2$ or a compact smooth surface. Suppose that $S_j\to\infty$ and that $\mu_j$ are nonnegative Borel measures on $X$ with $\mu_j(X)\le 1$. There are a subsequence and an upper semicontinuous function $V\colon X\to[-\infty,0]$ such that, for every compact $F\subset X$ and every open $G\subset X$, $$\label{eq:profile-bounds}
 \limsup_{j\to\infty}\frac{\log\mu_j(F)}{2S_j}\le\sup_F V,
 \qquad
 \liminf_{j\to\infty}\frac{\log\mu_j(G)}{2S_j}\ge\sup_G V.$$ Here $\log 0=\sup\varnothing=-\infty$. More generally, if continuous real functions $f_j$ converge locally uniformly to $f$, then $$\begin{aligned}
 \limsup_{j\to\infty}\frac{1}{2S_j}
       \log\int_F e^{2S_j f_j}\,d\mu_j&\le\sup_F(V+f),\\
 \liminf_{j\to\infty}\frac{1}{2S_j}
       \log\int_G e^{2S_j f_j}\,d\mu_j&\ge\sup_G(V+f).
 \end{aligned}$$ The same subsequence works for all these sets and weights.

There is a fixed $L>0$ such that, at any nonterminal square $Q$, if $N(Q)>L$ or $\abs{b}>L$, one can assign a tilt $b_q$ to each child $q$ so that, writing $m=m(Q,b)$, $m'=m(q,b_q)$, and $b'=b_q$, one has $$\label{eq:descent}
 \begin{gathered}
 \mathbb E\bigl(\abs{b'-b}\mid Q,b\bigr)\le C_1(m+f_Q),
 \qquad
 \mathbb E\bigl(m'\mid Q,b\bigr)\le \tfrac12(m+f_Q),\\
 f_Q=\frac{C_0}{K_Q}+s(Q).
 \end{gathered}$$ Moreover, if $Q_0=I,Q_1,\ldots,Q_d$ is any path from $I$ to the terminal level, then $$\label{eq:floors}
 \sum_{i=0}^{d}f_{Q_i}
 \le \frac{A}{A-1}
       \left(\frac{C_0}{a_0}+s(I)\right).$$

Start at $I$ with a prescribed tilt $b_I$. Whenever a step is made, choose the child tilts by Lemma <a href="#lem:descent" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:descent">11</a>, and suppose that its applicability condition holds at every square from which the procedure continues. Stop at a time $T$, no later than the terminal level. Write $m_i=m(Q_i,b_i)$ for the visited values, including the last value $m_T$. There is a constant $C_{\mathrm{st}}$, independent of the depth and of the stopping rule, such that $$\label{eq:stopped-descent}
 \mathbb E\left[
      \sum_{i=0}^{T}m_i
      +\sum_{i=0}^{T-1}\abs{b_{i+1}-b_i}
    \right]
 \le C_{\mathrm{st}}\bigl(m(I,b_I)+1\bigr).$$ The second sum includes the step producing the last square, even if that square causes the procedure to stop.

For the fixed $L$ in Lemma <a href="#lem:descent" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:descent">11</a>, there are constants $B_*\ge1$ and $\eta_*>0$ such that every admissible square with $s(Q)\le1$ and $K_Q\ge a_0$ satisfies $$\label{eq:large-slope}
 \abs{b}\ge B_*,
 \qquad m(Q,b)\le\eta_*\abs{b}
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad N(Q)>L.$$

There are a finite threshold $H$, a number $\delta>0$, and an integer $J\ge0$ such that every entry $Q$ admits a measurable set $Y_Q\subset D_*Q$, where $D_*=20A$, with $$\label{eq:persistence}
 \begin{split}
 |Y_Q|&\ge\delta|Q|,\\
 \#\{F\in\mathcal E:\ s(F)<s(Q),\ y\in F\}&\le J
 \qquad\text{for almost every }y\in Y_Q.
 \end{split}$$ Only entries of the given tree are counted, including when $Y_Q$ extends outside the root.

